WEBVTT

00:00:01.048 --> 00:00:03.636
<v><b>P:</b></v> So, Janice, what’s your take on the book?

00:00:03.971 --> 00:00:07.817
<v><b>J:</b></v> Thanks, Sam. Well, this is a very interesting book.

00:00:07.939 --> 00:00:14.249
According to the authors, helicopter parents are a combination of strict and supportive.

00:00:14.654 --> 00:00:18.389
They have high expectations about what their children should achieve,

00:00:18.662 --> 00:00:21.499
both academically and in their general lives,

00:00:21.882 --> 00:00:26.955
but they are also available to talk to them and give them help when they need it.

00:00:27.399 --> 00:00:31.049
This is very different from authoritarian parents

00:00:31.446 --> 00:00:35.965
– the so-called tiger parents – who put a big emphasis on obedience.

00:00:36.366 --> 00:00:41.155
<v><b>P:</b></v> And what do the authors say is the main benefit of helicopter parenting?

00:00:41.408 --> 00:00:46.754
<v><b>J:</b></v> Well, the main benefit of this type of parenting is in academic achievement.

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:53.146
There are several studies that prove that children of helicopter parents get the highest exam results

00:00:53.551 --> 00:00:59.035
– regardless of background, they achieve higher scores in maths, reading, and science

00:00:59.345 --> 00:01:02.960
– and these children are also very likely to go on to university.

00:01:03.221 --> 00:01:07.644
<v><b>P:</b></v> So, how does this compare with other types of parenting? How do their children do?

00:01:07.953 --> 00:01:13.276
<v><b>J:</b></v> Well, apparently, children of free-range parents get the second-highest results,

00:01:13.488 --> 00:01:15.875
followed by children of tiger parents.

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:16.845
<v><b>P:</b></v> Which is interesting,

00:01:17.129 --> 00:01:21.826
because people normally think that children of tiger parents get the top results!

00:01:22.589 --> 00:01:26.782
So, what kinds of things do helicopter parents do with their children

00:01:27.114 --> 00:01:30.376
that make such a difference to academic performance?

00:01:30.643 --> 00:01:38.014
<v><b>J:</b></v> Well, reading with children, telling them stories, and, when they are older, discussing politics:

00:01:38.372 --> 00:01:44.076
these are all things which they say push up test scores significantly, and, apparently,

00:01:44.323 --> 00:01:49.294
doing all three regularly can account for a huge increase in test scores.

00:01:49.844 --> 00:01:54.733
But, in fact, they do say that it’s not so much the specific activity that makes a difference,

00:01:55.096 --> 00:01:58.856
it’s more that these parents are closely engaged with their children.

00:02:00.103 --> 00:02:03.896
They give them lots of attention – the right sort of attention.

00:02:03.919 --> 00:02:11.011
<v><b>P:</b></v> And they’re also very keen on extracurricular activities: the music lessons, the sports, and so on.

00:02:11.641 --> 00:02:13.129
Do they make a difference, too?

00:02:13.266 --> 00:02:16.970
<v><b>J:</b></v> Yes. Well, helicopter parents commonly insist

00:02:16.995 --> 00:02:22.176
that their children learn an instrument or a foreign language, or do a sport.

00:02:22.612 --> 00:02:24.340
This is significant, the authors say,

00:02:24.365 --> 00:02:30.222
because certain types of extracurricular activities have an important influence on a child’s development.

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:38.286
For example, encouraging a child to play the violin or piano is not just for the pleasure of music:

00:02:38.701 --> 00:02:41.888
it also trains them to focus better on a task.